Amendment: warm paper on the login and signup left column
(follow-on to home_page_rebuild_20260906, added to PR #39)

REQUESTED
Owner: 'Give the login and create account page left hand column the same background as
the Why we exist section on the home page and add it to this pr.'

WHAT CHANGED
  M app/login/page.tsx    left column #f5f3f3 -> #eee9df
  M app/signup/page.tsx   left column #f5f3f3 -> #eee9df

WHY A HEX LITERAL AND NOT THE TOKEN
Using bg-landing-warm would have been the better engineering choice on a themed page,
and it is not one here. Both auth pages set every colour through inline style objects —
16 on login, 17 on signup — and neither has a single dark: variant, so they render light
regardless of the active theme. A theme-aware column dropped into that would invert on
its own in dark mode while the sixteen colours around it stayed light. #eee9df is the
light value of --landing-warm, verified identical below, and it matches the convention
already in force in those two files.

The duplication is real and worth removing later by converting both pages to the
landing-* tokens wholesale. That is a behavioural change to the auth pages' dark
rendering, so it belongs in its own change rather than smuggled into this one.

VERIFICATION PERFORMED
  npx tsc --noEmit -> exit 0. Confirms the /* */ comment between JSX attributes parses;
    it is legal there, but worth checking rather than assuming.
  npm run build    -> exit 0, Compiled successfully in 7.7s, 39/39 static pages.
  CI=true npm test -> 17 files, 260 tests passed.
  Colour equality, measured rather than eyeballed. The computed background of the auth
    left column and the resolved value of --landing-warm serialise differently — the
    literal as rgb(), the token as lab() — so they were converted to a common space:
      --landing-warm  lab(92.5138 0.453621 5.34849)  -> rgb(238, 233, 223)
      auth column     #eee9df                        -> rgb(238, 233, 223)
    Identical.
  Visual, both pages, against the production build on :3100. The warm column now reads
    as a distinct panel against the #fbf9f9 form column; the previous #f5f3f3 was nearly
    indistinguishable from it.
